{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JGp7YAv2orrRLFXVBwt6nT","type":"tool_result","content":"147:<!-- FIGURE: ch08-fig01-kubectl-verb-resource-grammar -->\n148:```\n161:```\n163:**Figure 8.1 —** Five commands you already know, aligned on the same four slots. What to notice is the empty columns: `kubectl get pods` omits NAME and flags, and gets every Pod as a result; `kubectl cordon node-7` omits TYPE entirely, because the verb already implies what kind of thing it operates on. What to notice second is the asymmetry at the bottom, which is the examinable half of this section.\n265:<!-- FIGURE: ch08-fig02-three-api-gates -->\n266:```\n274:```\n276:**Figure 8.2 —** What to notice is the fourth arrow. Gates one and two have one way out other than forward: refusal. Gate three has two. It can refuse, or it can alter the request and let the altered version continue. The three questions, in order, are: *who are you*, *may you do this*, and *should this, as written, be allowed*.\n348:<!-- FIGURE: ch08-fig05-quota-vs-limitrange -->\n349:```\n363:```\n365:**Figure 8.3 —** What to notice is that the two panels fail *differently*. On the left, a boundary is drawn around the namespace and the fifth Pod is refused. On the right, there is no namespace boundary at all — the constraints sit on individual Pods — and the fifth Pod is not refused but modified. Scope is the discrimination; the two failure modes are the proof.\n469:<!-- FIGURE: ch08-fig04-node-lifecycle-cordon-drain -->\n470:```\n482:```\n484:**Figure 8.4 —** What to notice is what does *not* change between the first two panels. The three Pods aboard the cordoned node are still running, still serving, still entirely unaffected. Only the arriving Pod's fate differs. The node does not empty until `drain`.\n705:<!-- FIGURE: ch08-fig03-version-skew-window -->\n706:```\n719:```\n721:**Figure 8.5 —** The double line at 0 is the API server's minor version. For every component but one it is a wall: bars extend to the left and stop dead. `kubectl` is the single bar that crosses to the right. The axis is deliberately relative; the rules do not change when the version numbers do. Note that the HA kube-apiserver rule is not a bar here — it is a bound between two API servers rather than a bound relative to one.\n890:<!-- FIGURE: ch08-zenith-consequences-not-rules -->\n891:```\n904:```\n906:**Figure 8.6 —** What to notice is the chapter number beside each controller. This is Chapter 3's architecture diagram, unchanged, except that you have now put your own hands on the left-hand side of it, and every mechanism on the right-hand side is one you were taught somewhere in Part II. What to notice second is that no arrow on the left reaches a controller directly. There are no side channels."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4b5ac540-28c4-4315-95f0-6cf7711fa549","uuid":"3d777264-f24e-477d-bd15-9433810a6ff3","timestamp":"2026-08-31T03:21:59.912Z","tool_use_result":{"mode":"content","numFiles":0,"filenames":[],"content":"147:<!-- FIGURE: ch08-fig01-kubectl-verb-resource-grammar -->\n148:```\n161:```\n163:**Figure 8.1 —** Five commands you already know, aligned on the same four slots. What to notice is the empty columns: `kubectl get pods` omits NAME and flags, and gets every Pod as a result; `kubectl cordon node-7` omits TYPE entirely, because the verb already implies what kind of thing it operates on. What to notice second is the asymmetry at the bottom, which is the examinable half of this section.\n265:<!-- FIGURE: ch08-fig02-three-api-gates -->\n266:```\n274:```\n276:**Figure 8.2 —** What to notice is the fourth arrow. Gates one and two have one way out other than forward: refusal. Gate three has two. It can refuse, or it can alter the request and let the altered version continue. The three questions, in order, are: *who are you*, *may you do this*, and *should this, as written, be allowed*.\n348:<!-- FIGURE: ch08-fig05-quota-vs-limitrange -->\n349:```\n363:```\n365:**Figure 8.3 —** What to notice is that the two panels fail *differently*. On the left, a boundary is drawn around the namespace and the fifth Pod is refused. On the right, there is no namespace boundary at all — the constraints sit on individual Pods — and the fifth Pod is not refused but modified. Scope is the discrimination; the two failure modes are the proof.\n469:<!-- FIGURE: ch08-fig04-node-lifecycle-cordon-drain -->\n470:```\n482:```\n484:**Figure 8.4 —** What to notice is what does *not* change between the first two panels. The three Pods aboard the cordoned node are still running, still serving, still entirely unaffected. Only the arriving Pod's fate differs. The node does not empty until `drain`.\n705:<!-- FIGURE: ch08-fig03-version-skew-window -->\n706:```\n719:```\n721:**Figure 8.5 —** The double line at 0 is the API server's minor version. For every component but one it is a wall: bars extend to the left and stop dead. `kubectl` is the single bar that crosses to the right. The axis is deliberately relative; the rules do not change when the version numbers do. Note that the HA kube-apiserver rule is not a bar here — it is a bound between two API servers rather than a bound relative to one.\n890:<!-- FIGURE: ch08-zenith-consequences-not-rules -->\n891:```\n904:```\n906:**Figure 8.6 —** What to notice is the chapter number beside each controller. This is Chapter 3's architecture diagram, unchanged, except that you have now put your own hands on the left-hand side of it, and every mechanism on the right-hand side is one you were taught somewhere in Part II. What to notice second is that no arrow on the left reaches a controller directly. There are no side channels.","numLines":24,"totalLines":24}}
